  "worked_solution": "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=y{e}^{x},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071034/assets/903b7f3c6bd9721a974160487ca5099fdf54ce02b61697a7830bb662b26d8953.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(y{e}^{x})=y{e}^{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(y{e}^{x})^{2}+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
